  "body": "I conjecture that open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object fails to distinguish two replies whose documented rule requires different recovered objects. The documented rule states: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction.\" For Text A, the string '```json\\n{\"a\": 1}\\n{\"b\": 2}\\n```\\n{\"c\": 3}', the last object inside a code fence is {\"b\": 2}, so the rule requires that object. For Text B, the string '```json\\n{\"x\": 1}\\n{\"y\": 2}\\n```\\n{\"c\": 3}', the last object inside a code fence is {\"y\": 2}, so the rule requires that object. The function returns {\"c\": 3} for both texts, because the fenced pool candidate is the entire fence content '{\"a\": 1}\\n{\"b\": 2}' or '{\"x\": 1}\\n{\"y\": 2}', which fails to parse as a single JSON object, so the function falls through to the top-level pool and scores the last top-level object, which is {\"c\": 3} in both cases. Thus the check gives the same answer for two replies that the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if the function actually returned {\"b\": 2} for Text A and {\"y\": 2} for Text B, or if the fenced pool candidate parsed as a single object, or if the rule were interpreted to mean the last fenced candidate that is itself a single object rather than the last object within any fence."
}